---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import TwoLayerModel from '../components/diagrams/TwoLayerModel.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
import NewsletterCTA from '../components/sections/NewsletterCTA.astro';

const talkingPoints = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'Agentic Developer Relations produces numbers leadership can act on.',
    body:
      'The scorecard starts with the First-Attempt Integration Success Rate: whether developers using AI tools succeed with the platform. The working hypothesis is that a higher rate leads to more adoption, and that link has to be shown with your own activation data. Even before it is, a number that moves when DevRel does its work changes the conversation with leadership.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Evaluation evidence wins the engineering conversation.',
    body:
      'When DevRel can show where agents fail on the platform, how much of their time goes to tools, and where developers have to step in, it has an engineering argument backed by traces and failure cases instead of anecdote.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'The evaluation suite is the institutional memory the field has needed.',
    body:
      'Senior DevRel practitioners carry enormous tacit knowledge about what a correct integration looks like, and it walks out the door when they leave. A suite of tasks and graders records it, and keeps working when tools and formats change.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Human community work keeps its value, and may feed the agent side.',
    body:
      'As generated content fills every channel, the patterns that need real human presence — conference sessions, office hours, community events — are the hardest to imitate. What people say in public may also influence what agents choose. That is a hypothesis worth testing, and a reason to keep the two kinds of work close.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'The work already exists. This gives it a name and a place.',
    body:
      'DevRel teams everywhere find some of their work has shifted toward AI tools, without a name, career path, or measurement framework. Engineering calls how agents fare on a platform Agent Experience. Agentic Developer Relations is the DevRel practice that works on it.',
  },
];

const objections = [
  {
    q: '"We already ship an MCP server."',
    a: 'An MCP server gives agents a way in. It does not tell you whether they choose the platform, succeed with it, or what that costs. Measure the first-attempt success rate with and without the server. It is one of several things to build, and like the others it has to be shown to help.',
  },
  {
    q: '"We don\'t have budget for new roles."',
    a: 'Start with one day per week from an existing practitioner, dedicated to running the evaluation. The first run tells you something you did not know, because nothing else in your dashboard looks for it. Whether it justifies more investment depends on what it finds.',
  },
  {
    q: '"Our developers don\'t use AI tools that heavily."',
    a: 'The silent majority is the measurement problem. Developers whose AI tools fail to integrate may never file a ticket, so you will not see it. Some will leave. Run one controlled test suite against your top five integration tasks and measure what you find. You will not know the number until you do.',
  },
  {
    q: '"This is an engineering problem, not a DevRel problem."',
    a: 'Much of it is. Engineering owns the API, sign-in, errors, and tooling. DevRel brings what engineering does not have: the list of things developers are trying to do, a judgment of whether the result is good, and the feedback loop to product. The work is shared, and the evidence is DevRel\'s part.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Agentic Developer Relations for DevRel Practitioners"
  description="What Agentic Developer Relations means for practicing DevRel professionals — five talking points, the objections you'll face, and what to do in week one."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">For DevRel practitioners</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">Agentic Developer Relations for DevRel Practitioners</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      The relevance crisis is real. Agentic Developer Relations is the
      part of the answer DevRel can own. Here's how to lead the
      conversation — and what to do in week one, with no new budget.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-12">
    <h2>The crisis you can feel but might not have named</h2>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Something has shifted in DevRel, and most practitioners can feel it
      without being able to name it precisely.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      AI tools are flooding every content channel. Documentation that took
      days to write can be generated in hours. Tutorials that required real
      technical expertise are being produced by developers who point AI
      tools at API references. Blog posts, guides, quick starts — the
      traditional content output of DevRel — are available at a price
      approaching zero.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      This doesn't make DevRel obsolete. But it does change where DevRel's
      value concentrates. The execution layer is being automated. The
      judgment layer is not. And a new audience has appeared that most DevRel
      programs aren't serving at all: the AI agents developers now work
      through. Those agents choose platforms, and either produce correct
      integrations or don't — silently, at scale, without generating any
      traditional feedback signals.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Agentic Developer Relations names the work that DevRel needs to do for
      that audience.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-10">
        <h2>Five talking points for your team and your leadership</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        {
          talkingPoints.map((t) => (
            <div class="border border-border rounded-xl bg-surface p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-primary font-bold text-sm mb-2">{t.n}</p>
              <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">
                {t.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">{t.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>Two linked kinds of work</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Agentic Developer Relations doesn't replace the human-facing work of DevRel.
        It runs alongside it, with its own metrics and methods, sharing
        platform expertise, leadership, and usually people. Community work
        keeps its own budget floor. The two are linked in both directions:
        agent failures tell you what to teach, and what the community
        writes in public is part of what agents learn from.
      </p>
    </div>
    <TwoLayerModel />
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Objections you'll face — and responses</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        {
          objections.map((o) => (
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber bg-amber-surface rounded-r-lg p-5">
              <p class="font-semibold text-heading">{o.q}</p>
              <p class="text-body mt-3 leading-relaxed">{o.a}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>What to do in week one</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        No new budget required. No new headcount required. One decision
        required: designate one practitioner as the responsible owner of
        measuring how agents fare on the platform. Give them one day per week. Ask them to
        do three things:
      </p>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 1">
        Write a list of the <strong>10 most common integration tasks</strong>
        developers ask for help with. These are your canonical tasks.
      </Callout>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 2">
        For each task, write the <strong>median prompt</strong> — what a
        reasonably competent developer would actually type into Copilot or
        Claude Code. Not the best prompt. The typical one.
      </Callout>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 3">
        Run those prompts through <strong>two AI tools</strong>. Grade each
        output against the four checks under "Controlled evaluation" on
        the <a href="/measurement/">Measurement page</a>, and record pass
        or fail for each.
      </Callout>

      <p class="mt-6">
        That's your baseline. You now have more signal about agent
        ecosystem health than anything in your current dashboard. Everything
        else — automated graders, more models, tested guidance for agents —
        is built on top of this baseline.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-site pb-20">
    <NewsletterCTA />
  </section>
</PageLayout>
